"Kiss from a Rose" was written in 1987, several years prior to the release of [[Seal (1991 album)|Seal's eponymous debut album]] from 1991. After writing the song, Seal felt "embarrassed by it" and "threw the tape in the corner". Seal did not present it to producer [[Trevor Horn]] until the recording sessions for ''Seal II''. In 2015, Seal said of the song: "To be honest, I was never really that proud of it, though I like what Trevor did with the recording. He turned that tape from my corner into another 8 million record sales and my name became a household name".<ref>{{cite web|title=Seal – Kiss From a Rose Lyrics|url=https://genius.com/8225372|publisher=[[Genius (website)|Genius]]|access-date=20 November 2015|date=18 November 2015}}</ref>

"Kiss from a Rose" was the second single taken from the ''[[Batman Forever]]'' film soundtrack, and topped the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] for one week in August 1995. It also went to number four on the [[UK Singles Chart]], where it had originally reached number 20 in 1994. It was also nominated for the [[MTV Movie Award for Best Song from a Movie]] in [[1996 MTV Movie Awards|1996]].<ref name="Songfacts">{{cite web|url=http://www.songfacts.com/detail.php?id=3821 |title=Kiss From A Rose by Seal Songfacts |publisher=Songfacts.com |access-date=2 January 2011}}</ref>

Seal talked about the long, strange journey that the song went through on ''The [[Brian McKnight]] Show'' season finale that aired 30 May 2010. He described how the song initially dropped out of the charts shortly after its release. [[Joel Schumacher]] subsequently called Seal, and requested use of the song to play over a love scene between the characters played by [[Nicole Kidman]] and [[Val Kilmer]] in ''Batman Forever''.{{citation needed|date=September 2025}}

